---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import LeafControlDiagram from '../../components/LeafControlDiagram.astro';
import LeafSignalDiagram from '../../components/LeafSignalDiagram.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'leaf-me-alone');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing project entry: leaf-me-alone');
---

<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="Water takes time to percolate to the sensor, so an irrigation controller that reads immediately after watering reads a stale value and overwaters. This system was designed around that delay instead of around a threshold."
>
  <h2>Context and constraints</h2>

  <p>
    A closed-loop autonomous irrigation system built for a sensing and signal
    processing course, running on a single microcontroller with the sensors
    available in the course kit as the starting point. The target was a system
    that keeps a potted plant inside a moisture band without supervision and
    without overwatering it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The physical constraint that defines the problem is percolation delay. Water
    delivered at the surface takes time to reach the depth the probe sits at. A
    controller that thresholds a raw reading and runs the pump until the reading
    crosses will therefore keep pumping against a measurement that has not yet
    responded, and the plant is flooded by the time the sensor agrees. Any design
    that ignores the delay fails the same way regardless of how good the sensor
    is.
  </p>

  <h2>Role</h2>

  <p>
    A three-person team. I worked on the sensor characterization studies, the
    signal chain and calibration, and the control state machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements decomposition</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Requirements and how each was verified"
    columns={[{ head: 'Requirement' }, { head: 'Target' }, { head: 'Verification' }]}
    rows={[
      ['Resolve soil moisture change', 'below 1 percent VWC sample-to-sample', 'repeat sampling in a stable environment'],
      ['Reservoir level accuracy', 'within +/-5 mm', '5 known heights, 3 readings each'],
      ['Reach target moisture', 'within +/-5 percent VWC', '10 integrated watering trials'],
      ['Never overwater', 'hard pulse cap enforced', 'sensor isolated from delivered water'],
      ['Never dry-run the pump', 'inhibit below reservoir floor', 'drain and refill cycle'],
      ['Control latency', 'bounded and known', 'fixed 500 ms update interval'],
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Design decisions</h2>

  <h3>Sensor selection, decided by characterization rather than by datasheet</h3>

  <p>
    Two moisture-sensing approaches were available in the kit and both were
    evaluated experimentally before either was accepted or rejected.
  </p>

  <p>
    A capacitive touch sensor was proposed to infer moisture from oscillation
    period. It lost on two measurable grounds. The first is a timescale mismatch:
    soil moisture moves over hours to days through evaporation and uptake, and
    the sensor could not resolve gradual change between control updates, even
    though on the bench it cleanly separated fully saturated soil from fully dry
    soil. Separating the extremes is not the measurement the controller needs.
    The second is dynamic range: period variation between practical wet and dry
    conditions fell inside the sensor's own noise floor, so repeated trials at
    the same true moisture returned inconsistent readings.
  </p>

  <p>
    A piezoelectric element was proposed to read soil compaction as a dryness
    proxy. Testing showed why the premise does not hold: the element is a charge
    generator that responds to dynamic force, not to static load. Inserted and
    left stationary it produced a brief transient as it was pushed in and then
    settled to zero, regardless of moisture or compaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both were replaced with a dedicated capacitive soil moisture probe. That was
    chosen over the cheaper resistive style on a specific physical criterion:
    resistive probes pass DC through the soil and corrode by electrolysis, which
    makes them a consumable in exactly the deployment this system targets.
  </p>

  <h3>Pulse and soak rather than threshold and pump</h3>

  <p>
    The control strategy follows directly from the percolation constraint.
    Instead of running the pump until the reading crosses a target, the
    controller delivers a fixed short pulse, then waits for the water to reach
    the probe before it looks again. The measurement is only trusted after the
    system has given the physics time to happen. A hard cap on consecutive pulses
    bounds the worst case if the probe is ever isolated from the delivered water.
  </p>

  <h2>Implementation</h2>

  <h3>Signal chain and calibration</h3>

  <p>
    The probe's analog output is read on a 12-bit converter and passed through a
    Kalman filter with process and measurement noise tuned empirically, holding
    sample-to-sample variation under 1 percent volumetric water content in a
    stable environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Calibration endpoints were measured directly rather than assumed: 1210 counts
    with the probe submerged and 2523 counts in open air, a 1313-count dynamic
    range. Those endpoints map onto an approximate 10 to 50 percent volumetric
    water content scale for potting soil rather than a naive 0 to 100 percent
    scale, because 100 percent volumetric water content is physically impossible
    in soil, and a controller calibrated against an impossible endpoint is
    calibrated against nothing. The 10 to 50 percent range comes from published
    soil-science reference values for the medium.
  </p>

  <LeafSignalDiagram />

  <h3>Reservoir sensing</h3>

  <p>
    An ultrasonic rangefinder mounted above the reservoir is driven by a
    timer-based state machine with interrupt edge capture on the echo line. Time
    of flight converts to distance through a least-squares fit against measured
    known heights rather than through the nominal speed of sound, is smoothed
    with a four-sample moving average, and maps to a 0 to 100 percent level
    through a two-point field calibration.
  </p>

  <h3>Control</h3>

  <p>
    A four-state machine updates every 500 ms: calibrate, idle, water and soak.
    Idle reads moisture and reservoir level and enters watering only when the
    soil is below the trigger threshold and the reservoir is above its own floor,
    so a low reservoir inhibits the pump rather than dry-running it. Watering
    runs a single 500 ms pump pulse. Soaking waits 10 seconds for percolation,
    re-reads, and either returns to idle on reaching target or issues another
    pulse, with a hard cap of 10 pulses per cycle.
  </p>

  <LeafControlDiagram />

  <h2>Results</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Validation results"
    columns={[{ head: 'Test' }, { head: 'Result' }]}
    rows={[
      ['Reservoir level accuracy, 5 known heights, 3 readings each', 'all within +/-3 mm against a +/-5 mm goal'],
      ['Integrated watering, 10 trials', '10 of 10 reached target within +/-5 percent VWC'],
      ['Pulse efficiency', '9 of 10 trials converged on a single 500 ms pulse'],
      ['Overwatering cap', 'verified by isolating the sensor from delivered water; system halted at the cap'],
      ['Low-reservoir inhibit', 'pump refused to run below threshold, resumed after refill'],
      ['Sample-to-sample variation, stable environment', 'under 1 percent VWC'],
      ['Control latency', 'approximately 500 ms, bounded by the update interval'],
    ]}
    highlight={[[1, 1], [2, 1]]}
  />

  <p>
    That 9 of 10 trials converged on a single pulse is the result that says the
    pulse size and soak interval were matched to the soil, rather than the system
    simply iterating its way to target.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope of the measurement</h2>

  <p>
    Volumetric water content here is approximate. True calibration requires
    gravimetric oven-dry analysis, which was not performed, so the percentages
    are a repeatable index rather than an absolute soil physics measurement. The
    endpoints are specific to general potting soil and would move for a different
    medium. Moisture is sampled at a single point rather than across the root
    zone, so the controller regulates the soil around the probe and assumes the
    pot is representative. Stating those bounds is part of the result: a moisture
    number without them is not a measurement.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>
